#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147323 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "842d6fb8-d895-584f-9a49-5d90409f1ba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8533" :question/problem "Find the phase line of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y^2 + 2y.$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ y(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<       0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0, \\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,0).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) <       0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/6940ca72733614d6916831fc0316ae75ca0bb07f75082c36be3897ebf9fd55d5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147324 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "45254b75-3db0-5c50-970a-4eea7db883f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8534" :question/problem "The slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown below. What is the phase line of the given equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d5db048e8e777aa02eb9b3fe22c9279b831b8510125b40cd361ae330fd1371a8.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y = 0$ and $y = 3$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y \\in (0,3),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y) <  0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y \\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (3,\\infty),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y) >   0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) <          0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/09fbbcef280dc3d6c0b68e2de23643fae8cbda246863d1b1d325ccb1c6368b4d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/id #uuid "adebd9bd-412d-5e0e-ab21-0087a5c35d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8535" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown below. What is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5506c77f5f257d4198cc665b3af34b900d37704fa2f09a53197462df694d018d.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y) = 0$ are $y = -2$ and $y = 0.$ Therefore $y = -2, 0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y) >0$ when $y \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (-2,0).$\n\n- $f(y)<       0$ when $y \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) <            0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/id #uuid "fba1d686-de7e-5e8e-ae0b-1f4f04fa79ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8586" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d4aac304110ed4743b58ba786141cecc404791130bf35884f8fbefe94c18fbd6.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= y^2 -4$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4-y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= (y-2)^2$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2, \\infty).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering $y'= y^2 -4.$ \nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-2,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2,\\infty).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider $y'=4-y^2.$\nFirst, $f(y)=4-y^2,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\nHowever, we note that $f(y) <     0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider $y'=(y-2)^2.$ In this case, $f(y)= (y-2)^2,$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=2$ only.  Therefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607ed537-0e43-5503-b7c6-79a2337febbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317268} {:answer/id #uuid "8c11f0c0-3110-515c-ae31-c48f989c42d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317269} {:answer/id #uuid "a6050415-1d2c-5b54-ab42-a6c1cc271d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317270} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2eaeef-cade-599b-80f5-0f383e9a5007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317271} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c8670b-5695-5682-8d1e-e93412e4d882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6050415-1d2c-5b54-ab42-a6c1cc271d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317270} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9fbbcb-fed6-5dfd-b12f-79b853e0c472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baceb21e-e41f-5237-bc5c-3f49cd7cc51a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100569" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/375647b4e2527841b48da5405effe2f15502f1d972586556796a55bcff441b94.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-y$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)(y-2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=-y(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y-1)(y+1),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0,1$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y-2).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3bbe0d-b255-5cd5-8d01-30696d14b0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/4498fa4b0a837e3801d9426c19e484c9556f5672b729b8fe09786114ce268fd5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317275} {:answer/id #uuid "787ebf3f-31f5-50cd-9b2b-055bd597708e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/358a06e0dd970f5a3a55cf0a95d5b46a225367ca921e1604c31efc00d583be54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317276} {:answer/id #uuid "0016cb4a-4c51-53a7-aebb-0a416ca6388b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/6aaa46e13d05e7df28911859a5e9a00374d19d3a909133a36bd6d27943592f72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317277} {:answer/id #uuid "67c6eea0-cdad-5ef7-943c-db9dfe1f2673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/68bcb20ed42bd71dfcc3dde0817f20b7b624a731f814a07bc8c814ace05dcdc6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317278} {:answer/id #uuid "43d8cb66-b77a-59fe-bbe2-98cd24fddb14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/df45e8b99f26e3fa18365575151967eca4babc0a3ef94d3ce98c7fcd1c281ae5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0016cb4a-4c51-53a7-aebb-0a416ca6388b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/6aaa46e13d05e7df28911859a5e9a00374d19d3a909133a36bd6d27943592f72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317277} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18f1a9e-7954-5749-aba4-cbe1a1ecbbac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbf4179a-0070-590f-bb71-a7d40ff9e026" :question/math-academy-id "q-335797" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/57fd283dafc8ee7da736fd8b9dcccd22481d71fbf79533636ad6b2027776de37.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-2$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-2,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,3).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1373e779f491868cebc5b9954d055a7241e46d6f7a4ef7ba595b5c0687c270d3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e150f06-5a2e-54d6-adf8-d5eee9193604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317282} {:answer/id #uuid "88fdb0e8-d1c1-5113-9d35-0a5e3b890ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317283} {:answer/id #uuid "fef71e81-677d-5401-8928-edfaa0e712e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317284} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4cce43-034e-59b5-bfbe-addb925498f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317285} {:answer/id #uuid "216e7939-8db7-5f65-82f0-0226d4e689f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fef71e81-677d-5401-8928-edfaa0e712e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317284} :answer-field/id #uuid "da6c6872-c9a1-5a1c-9b40-82642c172e9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d40d6bd-1228-582c-b5cf-0bb8cd81bd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335824" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9052447a-5b9a-54b0-ae77-4d8bf171ab22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/2a04842b64d6b282ec0856e9f4fd8d7a5654319a9af73ab217091cb3af95fc07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317289} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc66e8c-8581-5394-ab55-2d69d4b59f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/aff15abfa4145efb37ed319424f89097a333fce28e7ca4d91713283a360f280b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317290} {:answer/id #uuid "422e6dae-495e-5601-91e9-f062d4acfc6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/8d72bdb44dff1b7d97a256e411a6696e3cf372b313774356037da137d03318d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317291} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce4f56b-d329-5b97-8654-7d148eaa62fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1b10afb2caebfdd7d804f96b4bea7c7ae5e77ac8c381b5ce88e9b4c4b776bd47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292} {:answer/id #uuid "f64272b9-7840-554f-9a42-b03cdddfab75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/aa22eb5ee0909281679861d59a269f079e8b596790390d036e53b65010dc70d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce4f56b-d329-5b97-8654-7d148eaa62fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1b10afb2caebfdd7d804f96b4bea7c7ae5e77ac8c381b5ce88e9b4c4b776bd47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a76267a-a6c0-5831-8601-82ec8b1d502d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5df75f-ce9d-5773-a201-b1413c58dd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-336034" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/e6a178edcc79e9e36553acf436209525b490dbefde06823d571f8e317dab0086.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-1$ and $y=1$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (1,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-1,1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis.\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions.\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0.$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0.$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/62b3d23025e8b2716dd584678f1aef2ba3132a66250d0999cb13a2281347ee89.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8217008c-e78f-5854-b64a-fa1d5f84ea22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146984} {:answer/id #uuid "84d36794-a995-5cbd-b629-6b67d05d6509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146985} {:answer/id #uuid "bd57204a-b736-5198-890e-62539ab1d6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146986} {:answer/id #uuid "da9c5be1-adb3-505f-ab53-5e6debb55739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146987} {:answer/id #uuid "22ec9143-2509-52ba-b5f2-9c7436af40ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22ec9143-2509-52ba-b5f2-9c7436af40ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96512-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146988} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fa03be-5c54-5c70-b87a-bf906225141f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a675b97b-da4f-5c4f-81ca-8978f13b7a4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96512" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nIn this case, we have \n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & >0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/bbb6defd12fdc683f2df111cce7e10807fd8d69877ddf12de7fa97d3e9d53781.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca501537-aa20-5be0-936b-cd7d439d0859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147002} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd22284-33e6-5c1f-b6c6-8ee3001024d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147003} {:answer/id #uuid "0af470b9-f6b3-5563-a24a-dae09098f144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147004} {:answer/id #uuid "37908528-5c8f-5ee5-b324-a9d00460c67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147005} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe1e0b-719a-5240-beab-79f1ed167aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0af470b9-f6b3-5563-a24a-dae09098f144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96515-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147004} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c555691-ad28-513f-97f9-df64d7f06299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79dc5bea-a8e5-5bec-830b-c8eb6fa1519c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96515" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/cbccf40aca753c4c23cc2b159697deca70b45844d2cbf458c9a04f6dc6d3026f.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y<-2.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in this region.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $-2<y<0$ or $y>0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in these regions.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f385aab-2bb7-5b71-af9b-efdb1a6e34f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317296} {:answer/id #uuid "6227d4db-2d85-5ae6-bb87-7af4c0231412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/66b90a2d1509ddb46e27d3e7164dbe1ab9b1f42aefd5df4b4482d8d2046f9bba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317297} {:answer/id #uuid "5a79aafe-faa3-508a-9358-c8a5afb65bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/7b948fa43dc2f8a60f8e8df9d02ae65245891ae9ddb34ab8928b9dc696e36cc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317298} {:answer/id #uuid "7768ede5-a578-5e04-b133-e77a9822e825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/a8ee4e28944f944050c9378f62c72924219efb44e6b26c51f8a8ebc6b9300528.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317299} {:answer/id #uuid "01111f29-7899-5ce6-92f0-42206a38c570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b24f89fa181f60b0b2d6fdf1800339e1c88a2eb8da3d1ed87e0cca34140e2479.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6227d4db-2d85-5ae6-bb87-7af4c0231412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/66b90a2d1509ddb46e27d3e7164dbe1ab9b1f42aefd5df4b4482d8d2046f9bba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317297} :answer-field/id #uuid "330365c6-c176-50db-a4f9-8b5fac5beb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c62935d-0410-56a9-a53f-6067ac5354c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-97917" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1bc88bc79f6cec9d8f2a9e44d397fda9d0a9a2cbe0691d542f25e2bdfa259dc1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-2,y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=-2,0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/66b90a2d1509ddb46e27d3e7164dbe1ab9b1f42aefd5df4b4482d8d2046f9bba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d4b0cd0-a814-5f9a-9adf-b6462915a7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differe