:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8efd0f52-3668-56af-a338-a15bca7d1730" :question/math-academy-id "q-97918" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/bf8eb89b9fc5e0fa09e783f5774282decc778b6fe8ddc18e1a47c71a68505734.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d970e9a23302139dfc40db62a8a2697f2a2d68b7523b40e0f8268b587eb904fb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e659feee-a68b-5954-8ea6-4db47e909ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/63814e39a9f58bbf87798408d44bc9f350b6ab8b090caf53154028ab06ab7395.png" :db/id #ed