512f-b461-afed85e08555" :question/math-academy-id "q-97925" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/43b6a6096842e30cb544049d5d82c9e9587e23c1f36614f28401dfdca9b5ff26.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$ Therefore $y=-1,1$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/fc4cd58a2c1a6e8147099455ca2439a9d7be7eb39c54f12387061bb6305e8f74.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2471b3-f95f-5648-9e74-e882a2f95839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-97931-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147038} {:answer/id #uuid "beab9141-b2e5-5409-9f60-6022a17f0d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-97931-a-5.png" 