Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0b3e273-b29b-58b9-a239-7792c0da668c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b123be00d466cf117b6c3f19376230e240a6ef0a64677b5d18457d43d736fd94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317338} {:answer/id #uuid "54fdd8ab-0cc4-551a-a448-188a36e01a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/51eb48f8ab301a9c6155da6b650e1641a236479e9a2cf8dcb089540adb39ca7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317339} {:answer/id #uuid "c00195b1-01d6-5ada-a6e8-08a3c68e71aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/a41d7dab8dafadd838fc55ccf0fbc8b24f126e632e35f83154d35945b0888fea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317340} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa9ecd0-b069-575e-81bc-37c015a3222d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/ec0a0af774522cdb4f167a05b4764092bc7b9d0008e9e43157ca1d3068ccac56.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317341} {:answer/id #uuid "b30c60ab-cd5e-5fcd-9ebd-1ad14c89eb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d87ea933266242b22ee93eb80b2a1ea4cb5147d3d33c5f7f9dc65c9e1f3a3271.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0b3e273-b29b-58b9-a239-7792c0da668c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b123be00d466cf117b6c3f19376230e240a6ef0a64677b5d18457d43d736fd94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317338} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cd3b8c-0784-5b5a-8b66-21a478f8d1c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06ad98a7-533f-59fe-919c-9f9dc27f7c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-98526" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}+10y-24?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+10y-24 & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-10y+24) & =0 \\\\ -(y-4)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =4,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (4,6),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b123be00d466cf117b6c3f19376230e240a6ef0a64677b5d18457d43d736fd94.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e55e872c-d5ab-57cc-a51b-ca5204f0c597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/8d7a4b4ac510e040a6d6ca87cf96e72c30a298ea450b0154d6080b9e73a19e8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317345} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1d0d7-5652-58e4-b75b-3e56d858eb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/638f98960fbcfac5d0f10385037b92479fdab4177811f578d89bd997d7db2353.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317346} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7f7d02-409d-5d17-9b43-2b7d18d815b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/99e226354d8d1c48e11545d818083eb378ab931fd536e9a0389141e474ab27ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317347} {:answer/id #uuid "24851259-9065-5ec4-b0b0-f12cca0d497c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/7f44aa29a8d99f144707d3126102ad633fd8b0f947ae16d862870444a5125793.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348} {:answer/id #uuid "84a73f7b-85a0-517c-b152-8ab8d7f729df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a73f7b-85a0-517c-b152-8ab8d7f729df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349} :answer-field/id #uuid "82693eab-3d78-5e9e-80a6-140728160721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "796023e8-480d-5ead-996f-d9e2e236082e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98527" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}-12y-35?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-12y-35 & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}+12y+35) & =0 \\\\ -(y+7)(y+5) & =0 \\\\ y & =-7,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-7$ and $y=-5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,-5)$ | $(-5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+7)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y+5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-7,-5),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-7)\\cup (-5,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b08c48590fe2538666e0e3a559c123b0f5cd3bb35680f2c98d3c14691780a575.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56306bdf-f2c3-5918-b091-d5d3b736c7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} {:answer/id #uuid "4259bf83-670c-52f6-a8c7-60156b986676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/66b90a2d1509ddb46e27d3e7164dbe1ab9b1f42aefd5df4b4482d8d2046f9bba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317353} {:answer/id #uuid "61fe5d4f-501e-54d9-b87c-58411e399634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/7b948fa43dc2f8a60f8e8df9d02ae65245891ae9ddb34ab8928b9dc696e36cc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354} {:answer/id #uuid "2921b1b5-281b-5558-8f82-f36b685da1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/a8ee4e28944f944050c9378f62c72924219efb44e6b26c51f8a8ebc6b9300528.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355} {:answer/id #uuid "594ac190-2117-53b0-b849-3cfc089ea672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/fca94bef82d6e693a93796fe353fc0efacbba86f6b51c59d1fe7df7932ffd912.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56306bdf-f2c3-5918-b091-d5d3b736c7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} :answer-field/id #uuid "73bc13db-b415-5dae-a536-437f5ab0eb26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4c26980-2b20-50dd-83dd-2c79a12fb753" :question/math-academy-id "q-98556" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-4y?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the function $f(y),$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nIn this case, we have \n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & >0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04100966-9262-59ac-9b02-6985976876fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/286e8e35c0fcef26dfd4c417fafbf716b4461a1cb5124afcd4f30616e5baf353.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317359} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4bff38-3218-56e4-93a0-5e805b2897e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b9d19d704ebf663062f83802c8d6f364b7dbf131a2e67ce01454b2271fa5596f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317360} {:answer/id #uuid "a53004d1-0c39-50df-a6e5-55f6b9a30247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/952da77a48d78f706c652e9f3e4fa33381d872153be53d754928704416836c2e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317361} {:answer/id #uuid "c328045a-eda9-5051-8ac8-9f4b33c461cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/aac7a845d18b3721e6a643694d08cf0eecad71c6eaa0b845d292a016d36737f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317362} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0f0e1a-d875-571e-ba4f-2f1264efd455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/53e84723bb6734e917020241268dcf350dafab0a8739fa6f2d40fc508bb70820.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a0f0e1a-d875-571e-ba4f-2f1264efd455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/53e84723bb6734e917020241268dcf350dafab0a8739fa6f2d40fc508bb70820.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317363} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af99637-7ef4-596f-a541-c7483b85456f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f4cab14-3e93-532b-a75b-97642fb91bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-98558" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the function $f(y),$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -{y}^{2}(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-{y}^{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1de4442141a2555ebb9d7c9d4e29e0555566b5177bcd4166e59331459b8621aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c40349-53f1-5999-bd42-a070b7950fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317366} {:answer/id #uuid "653340e9-73be-57cc-b860-01b9f214c5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} {:answer/id #uuid "dd43307c-5510-58b9-a175-23eae1335632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317368} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a49b1e-c1c3-5053-abdc-f4f1198955a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317369} {:answer/id #uuid "900cb579-beb3-599d-a0ef-125010a02ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "653340e9-73be-57cc-b860-01b9f214c5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dde0b73-8f23-5261-9223-afac905fa2ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a946ccf-7250-5f83-ba8c-44f2d4585333" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26986fa5-9fe6-53ea-b7d8-7162fe332e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317373} {:answer/id #uuid "12a29b97-af35-541a-83a0-a26b96ed2f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317374} {:answer/id #uuid "0dda8671-52b1-53eb-ab1b-0f9c50326a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317375} {:answer/id #uuid "248e9fb2-0278-5d3f-bde0-b6314b89d0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317376} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa42dce-003e-5f69-b01c-aaaca30ee462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa42dce-003e-5f69-b01c-aaaca30ee462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317377} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bf5d8f-985a-58c1-ac95-46d283e15e25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d698ab4a-533d-5805-b371-ab030fdf00d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f56b8d-796a-5562-8393-f9d46e997cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380} {:answer/id #uuid "1430de37-6fec-5609-b7ba-36028c5541d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317381} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8d8d0f-9939-54c2-aa85-c59add9750fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382} {:answer/id #uuid "baa80c54-0a21-5c23-b55b-212d2a98d13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317383} {:answer/id #uuid "2204042e-ba29-54de-a806-c9c98d4df02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa80c54-0a21-5c23-b55b-212d2a98d13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317383} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f85cd1-c981-5424-bb07-e79adc1aa5cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d288e73b-370f-5ae4-a77c-c83deeee0c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
